A response matrix describes what each channel can observe (@cm-aerosol-response-matrix: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../sources/Mobility-Particle-Size-Spectrometers-Wiedensohler.lingua#r_1W06WPADV43B98EF8NSW9RDCWM", #source "../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP", #clean-manufacturing "../../../inquiry/metrology/uncertainty/Inverse-and-Bayesian-models.lingua#r_1TSJYTGBD1NY9Q7VP0BDFXZ94K") { r_4X7ZW1THRHMVFBYWCHKDHXXMYT
**Read:** [Mobility particle size spectrometers — Wiedensohler and colleagues](../sources/Mobility-Particle-Size-Spectrometers-Wiedensohler.lingua#r_1W06WPADV43B98EF8NSW9RDCWM), inversion principles and comparisons; [Calibration of aerosol instruments — Chen, Fletcher and Cheng](../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP), response and calibration design.

Discretize the population into concentration vector c. For counts in observation channels, write expected counts λ = Ac + b. Matrix element Aij has units of sampled volume times the probability that a particle in population bin j reaches and registers in channel i. It can include charge fractions, transport, selection and detection. Define disjoint versus cumulative channels before assigning a probability model.

For an original two-bin example, let effective sampled volume be 1 cm³, b = 0 and response rows be (0.8, 0.1) and (0.2, 0.7). Concentrations (100, 50) cm⁻³ give expected counts (85, 55). The response columns each sum to at most one for mutually exclusive detection outcomes, with the remainder representing missed events. This column constraint does not apply to cumulative channels that deliberately count an event more than once.

Size-bin width is part of the definition. Concentration per bin and concentration per ln(d) interval differ by that interval's width. Multiplying a density by a linear diameter width while labelling it logarithmic changes the recovered inventory.

**Work:** Build A for a three-bin hypothetical instrument, stating every factor and unit. Forward-predict two contrasting populations. Check total counts, missing fractions and zero-efficiency limits before attempting an inverse. Identify which corrections are already contained in A so they are not applied again to the inferred distribution.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
